" (https://gallium.inria.fr/~huet/PUBLIC/zip.pdf)\nciteturn97view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 6; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://gallium.inria.fr/~huet/PUBLIC/zip.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 206\nL0@P0: J. Functional Programming 1 (1): 1–000, January 1993\nL1@P0: c 1993 Cambridge University Press 1\nL2@P0: FUN C T I ONA L P EA R L S\nL3@P0: The Zipper\nL4@P0: G´erard Huet\nL5@P0: INRIA Rocquencourt, France\nL6@P0: Capsule Review\nL7@P0: Almost every programmer has faced the problem of representing a tree together with a\nL8@P0: subtree that is the focus of attention, where that focus may move left, right, up, or down\nL9@P0: the tree. The Zipper is Huet’s nifty name for a nifty data structure which fulfills this need.\nL10@P0: I wish I had known of it when I faced this task, because the solution I came up with was\nL11@P0: not quite so efficient or elegant as the Zipper.\nL12@P0: Introduction\nL13@P0: The main drawback to the purely applicative paradigm of programming is that\nL14@P0: many efficient algorithms use destructive operations in data structures such as bit\nL15@P0: vectors or character arrays or other mutable hierarchical classification structures,\nL16@P0: which are not immediately modeled as purely applicative data structures. A well\nL17@P0: known solution to this problem is called functional arrays(Paulson, 1991). For trees,\nL18@P0: this amounts to modifying an occurrence in a tree non-destructively by copying its\nL19@P0: path from the root of the tree. This is considered tolerable when the data structure\nL20@P0: is just an object local to some algorithm, the cost being logarithmic compared to\nL21@P0: the naive solution which copies all the tree. But when the data structure represents\nL22@P0: some global context, such as the buffer of a text editor or the data base of axioms\nL23@P0: and lemmas in a proof system, this technique is prohibitive. In the current note,\nL24@P0: we explain a simple solution, where tree editing is completely local, the handle on\nL25@P0: the data being not the original root of the tree, but rather the current position in\nL26@P0: the tree.\nL27@P0: The basic idea is simple: the tree is turned inside-out like a returned glove,\nL28@P0: pointers from the root to the current position being reversed in a path structure.\nL29@P0: The current location holds both the downward current subtree, and the upward\nL30@P0: path. All navigation and modification primitives operate on the location structure.\nL31@P0: Going up and down in the structure is analogous to closing and opening a zipper\nL32@P0: in a piece of clothing, whence the name.\nL33@P0: The author coined this data-type when designing the core of a structured editor\nL34@P0: for use as structure manager for a proof assistant. This simple idea must have been\nL35@P0-1: invented at numerous occasions by creative programmers, and the only justification2 G´erard Huet\nL36@P1: for presenting what ought to be folklore is that it does not appear to have been\nL37@P1: published, or even to be well-known.\nL38@P1: 1 The Zipper data structure\nL39@P1: There are many variations on the basic idea. First let us present a version which\nL40@P1: pertains to trees with variadic arity anonymous tree nodes, and tree leaves injecting\nL41@P1: values from an unspecified item type.\nL42@P1: 1.1 Trees, paths and locations\nL43@P1: We assume a type parameter item of the elements we want to manipulate hierar\u0002chically; the tree structure is just hierarchical lists grouping trees in a section. For\nL44@P1: instance, in the UNIX file system, items would be files and sections would be direc\u0002tories; in a text editor items would be characters, and two levels of sections would\nL45@P1: represent the buffer as a list of lines and lines as lists of characters. Generalising\nL46@P1: this to any level, we would get a notion of hierarchical Turing machine, where a\nL47@P1: tape position may contain either a symbol or a tape from a lower level.\nL48@P1: All algorithms presented here are written concretely in the programming language\nL49@P1: OCaml(Leroy et al., 1996). This code is translatable easily in any programming\nL50@P1: language, functional or not, lazy or not.\nL51@P1: type tree =\nL52@P1: Item of item\nL53@P1: | Section of tree list;;\nL54@P1: We now consider a path in a tree:\nL55@P1: type path =\nL56@P1: Top\nL57@P1: | Node of tree list * path * tree list;;\nL58@P1: A path is like a zipper, allowing one to rip the tree structure down to a certain\nL59@P1: location. A Node(l,p,r) contains its list l of elder siblings (starting with the\nL60@P1: eldest), its father path p, and its list of younger siblings (starting with the youngest).\nL61@P1: Note: a tree presented by a path has sibling trees, uncle trees, great-uncle trees,\nL62@P1: etc, but its father is a path, not a tree like in usual graph editors.\nL63@P1: A location in the tree adresses a subtree, together with its path.\nL64@P1: type location = Loc of tree * path;;\nL65@P1: A location consists of a distinguished tree, the current focus of attention, and\nL66@P1: its path, representing its surrounding context. Remark that a location does not\nL67@P1: correspond to an occurrence in the tree, as assumed for instance in term rewriting\nL68@P1: theory (Huet, 1980) or in tree editors (Donzeau-Gouge et al., 1984). It is rather a\nL69@P1: pointer to the arc linking the designated subtree to the surrouding context.\nL70@P1: Example. Assume we consider the parse tree of arithmetic expressions, with\nL71@P1-2: string items. The expression a × b + c × d parses as the tree:Functional pearls 3\nL72@P2: Section[Section[Item \"a\"; Item \"*\"; Item \"b\"];\nL73@P2: Item \"+\";\nL74@P2: Section[Item \"c\"; Item \"*\"; Item \"d\"]];;\nL75@P2: The location of the second multiplication sign in the tree is:\nL76@P2: Loc(Item \"*\",\nL77@P2: Node([Item \"c\"],\nL78@P2: Node([Item \"+\"; Section [Item \"a\"; Item \"*\"; Item \"b\"]],\nL79@P2: Top,\nL80@P2: []),\nL81@P2: [Item \"d\"]))\nL82@P2: 1.2 Navigation primitives in trees\nL83@P2: let go_left (Loc(t,p)) = match p with\nL84@P2: Top -> failwith \"left of top\"\nL85@P2: | Node(l::left,up,right) -> Loc(l,Node(left,up,t::right))\nL86@P2: | Node([],up,right) -> failwith \"left of first\";;\nL87@P2: let go_right (Loc(t,p)) = match p with\nL88@P2: Top -> failwith \"right of top\"\nL89@P2: | Node(left,up,r::right) -> Loc(r,Node(t::left,up,right))\nL90@P2: | _ -> failwith \"right of last\";;\nL91@P2: let go_up (Loc(t,p)) = match p with\nL92@P2: Top -> failwith \"up of top\"\nL93@P2: | Node(left,up,right) -> Loc(Section((rev left) @ (t::right)),up);;\nL94@P2: let go_down (Loc(t,p)) = match t with\nL95@P2: Item(_) -> failwith \"down of item\"\nL96@P2: | Section(t1::trees) -> Loc(t1,Node([],p,trees))\nL97@P2: | _ -> failwith \"down of empty\";;\nL98@P2: Note: all navigation primitives take constant time, except go_up, which is pro\u0002portional to the ‘juniority’ of the current term list_length(left).\nL99@P2: We may program with these primitives the access to the nth son of the current\nL100@P2: tree.\nL101@P2: let nth loc = nthrec\nL102@P2: where rec nthrec = function\nL103@P2: 1 -> go_down(loc)\nL104@P2: | n -> if n>0 then go_right(nthrec (n-1))\nL105@P2: else failwith \"nth expects a positive integer\";;\nL106@P2: 1.3 Changes, insertions and deletions\nL107@P2-3: We may mutate the structure at the current location as a local operation:4 G´erard Huet\nL108@P3: let change (Loc(_,p)) t = Loc(t,p);;\nL109@P3: Insertion to the left or to the right is natural and cheap.\nL110@P3: let insert_right (Loc(t,p)) r = match p with\nL111@P3: Top -> failwith \"insert of top\"\nL112@P3: | Node(left,up,right) -> Loc(t,Node(left,up,r::right));;\nL113@P3: let insert_left (Loc(t,p)) l = match p with\nL114@P3: Top -> failwith \"insert of top\"\nL115@P3: | Node(left,up,right) -> Loc(t,Node(l::left,up,right));;\nL116@P3: let insert_down (Loc(t,p)) t1 = match t with\nL117@P3: Item(_) -> failwith \"down of item\"\nL118@P3: | Section(sons) -> Loc(t1,Node([],p,sons));;\nL119@P3: We may also want to implement a deletion primitive. We may choose to move\nL120@P3: right, if possible, otherwise left, and up in case of an empty list.\nL121@P3: let delete (Loc(_,p)) = match p with\nL122@P3: Top -> failwith \"delete of top\"\nL123@P3: | Node(left,up,r::right) -> Loc(r,Node(left,up,right))\nL124@P3: | Node(l::left,up,[]) -> Loc(l,Node(left,up,[]))\nL125@P3: | Node([],up,[]) -> Loc(Section[],up);;\nL126@P3: We remark that delete is not such a simple operation.\nL127@P3: We believe that the set of datatypes and operations above is adequate for pro\u0002gramming the kernel of a structure editor in an applicative albeit efficient manner.\nL128@P3: 2 Variations on the basic idea\nL129@P3: 2.1 Scars\nL130@P3: When an algorithm has frequent operations which necessitate going up in the tree,\nL131@P3: and down again at the same position, it is a loss of time (and space, and garbage\u0002collecting time, etc) to close the sections in the meantime. It may be advantageous\nL132@P3: to leave “scars” in the structure allowing direct access to the memorized visited\nL133@P3: positions. Thus we replace the (non-empty) sections by triples memorizing a tree\nL134@P3: and its siblings:\nL135@P3: type memo_tree =\nL136@P3: Item of item\nL137@P3: | Siblings of memo_tree list * memo_tree * memo_tree list;;\nL138@P3: type memo_path =\nL139@P3: Top\nL140@P3: | Node of memo_tree list * memo_path * memo_tree list;;\nL141@P3-4: type memo_location = Loc of memo_tree * memo_path;;Functional pearls 5\nL142@P4: We show the simplified up and down operations on these new structures:\nL143@P4: let go_up_memo (Loc(t,p)) = match p with\nL144@P4: Top -> failwith \"up of top\"\nL145@P4: | Node(left,p’,right) -> Loc(Siblings(left,t,right),p’);;\nL146@P4: let go_down_memo (Loc(t,p)) = match t with\nL147@P4: Item(_) -> failwith \"down of item\"\nL148@P4: | Siblings(left,t’,right) -> Loc(t’,Node(left,p,right));;\nL149@P4: We leave it to the reader to adapt other primitives.\nL150@P4: 2.2 First-order terms\nL151@P4: So far, our structures are completely untyped, our tree nodes are not even labelled.\nL152@P4: We have a kind of structured editor `a la LISP, but oriented more toward “splicing”\nL153@P4: operations than the usual rplaca and rplacd primitives.\nL154@P4: If we want to implement a tree-manipulation editor for abstract-syntax trees, we\nL155@P4: have to label our tree nodes with operator names. If we use items for this pur\u0002pose, this suggests the usual LISP encoding of first-order terms: F(T1, ..., Tn) being\nL156@P4: coded as the tree Section[Item(F); T1; ... Tn]. A dual solution is suggested by\nL157@P4: combinatory logic, where the comb-like structure respects the application ordering:\nL158@P4: [Tn; ... T1; Item(F)]. Neither of these solutions respects arity however.\nL159@P4: We shall not pursue details of such generic variations any more, but rather con\u0002sider how to adapt the idea to a specific given signature of operators given with\nL160@P4: their arities, in such a way that tree edition maintains well-formedness of the tree\nL161@P4: according to arities.\nL162@P4: Basically, to each constructor F of the signature with arity n we associate n path\nL163@P4: operators Node(F, i), with 1 ≤ i ≤ n, each of arity n, used when going down the\nL164@P4: i-th subtree of an F-term. More precisely, Node(F, i) has one path argument and\nL165@P4: n − 1 tree arguments holding the current siblings.\nL166@P4: We show for instance the structure corresponding to binary trees:\nL167@P4: type binary_tree =\nL168@P4: Nil\nL169@P4: | Cons of binary_tree * binary_tree;;\nL170@P4: type binary_path =\nL171@P4: Top\nL172@P4: | Left of binary_path * binary_tree\nL173@P4: | Right of binary_tree * binary_path;;\nL174@P4: type binary_location = Loc of binary_tree * binary_path;;\nL175@P4-5: let change (Loc(_,p)) t = Loc(t,p);;6 G´erard Huet\nL176@P5: let go_left (Loc(t,p)) = match p with\nL177@P5: Top -> failwith \"left of top\"\nL178@P5: | Left(father,right) -> failwith \"left of Left\"\nL179@P5: | Right(left,father) -> Loc(left,Left(father,t));;\nL180@P5: let go_right (Loc(t,p)) = match p with\nL181@P5: Top -> failwith \"right of top\"\nL182@P5: | Left(father,right) -> Loc(right,Right(t,father))\nL183@P5: | Right(left,father) -> failwith \"right of Right\";;\nL184@P5: let go_up (Loc(t,p)) = match p with\nL185@P5: Top -> failwith \"up of top\"\nL186@P5: | Left(father,right) -> Loc(Cons(t,right),father)\nL187@P5: | Right(left,father) -> Loc(Cons(left,t),father);;\nL188@P5: let go_first (Loc(t,p)) = match t with\nL189@P5: Nil -> failwith \"first of Nil\"\nL190@P5: | Cons(left,right) -> Loc(left,Left(p,right));;\nL191@P5: let go_second (Loc(t,p)) = match t with\nL192@P5: Nil -> failwith \"second of Nil\"\nL193@P5: | Cons(left,right) -> Loc(right,Right(left,p));;\nL194@P5: Efficient destructive algorithms on binary trees may be programmed with these\nL195@P5: completely applicative primitives, which all use constant time, since they all reduce\nL196@P5: to local pointer manipulation.\nL197@P5: References\nL198@P5: V. Donzeau-Gouge, G. Huet, G. Kahn, B. Lang. “Programming Environments based on\nL199@P5: Structured Editors: The MENTOR Experience.” In Interactive Programming En\u0002vironments. Eds. Barstow D., Shrobe H. and Sandewall E., McGraw Hill, 1984, pp.\nL200@P5: 128-140.\nL201@P5: G. Huet. “Confluent Reductions: Abstract Properties and Applications to Term Rewriting\nL202@P5: Systems.” J. Assoc. Comp. Mach. 27,4 (1980) 797–821.\nL203@P5: X. Leroy, D. R´emy, J. Vouillon. “The Objective Caml system, documenta\u0002tion and user’s manual - release 1.02.” INRIA, oct. 1996. Available on\nL204@P5: ftp.inria.fr:INRIA/Projects/cristal.\nL205@P5: L. C. Paulson. “ML for the working programmer.” Cambridge University Press, 1991.\n"
